Always start by reading `{{ plans_root_in_worktree }}/intent/001_idea.md`.
If `{{ plans_root_in_worktree }}/index_of_decisions.md` exists, read it next when planning or reviewing;
when implementing, consult it if the plan leaves something unclear.
Read its header, Plan summaries and Terminology, list the topics with `grep -n '^###'`,
then read the topic sections relevant to the task (and Deferred work when planning).

The workflow below is enacted by the human user. Agents do not invoke `workflow`
subcommands themselves; they receive rendered prompts in the chat via tmux, appearing as ordinary user messages.
Script subcommands are not visible to the agents.
These workflow rules take precedence over any repo-local `AGENTS.md` instructions.
Manual steps are free-form chat, possibly multiple turns, or omitted entirely.
This listing exists so an agent knows in advance which state a plan is in,
which actions are expected in that state, and which actions belong to other states.
For example, an agent reading a plan in the `planning` state must not start writing code;
an agent in the `implementing` state must not rewrite the plan.

The user may deviate from the workflow at any time.
A prompt command may therefore arrive in a state where it is not listed below
(for example, `ask` may be sent in any state).
When this happens, follow the prompt as given.

Plans reside in `{{ plans_root_in_worktree }}`.
The plan state is defined by location, e.g. `{{ plans_root_in_worktree }}/implementing/002_foo.md` is in the state `implementing`.
Plan names consist of an unique sequece number, a slug, and the `.md` suffix.
Reviews (in {{ plans_root_in_worktree }}/reviews) do not follow this naming scheme.

- Record a decision when the user answers a question, picks between options, or resolves a critique point.
  Record it in the plan file right away, not only in chat.
- Decisions are recorded as a single numbered `## Decisions` list per file.
  Don't create decision-like sections labeled e.g. "Clarifications" or "Post-review fixes"; these go under "Decisions".
- Decisions made while fixing review findings go into the plan's Decisions list, not into the review.
- The list is append-only: never renumber. Amend in place and mark it amended;
  a withdrawn decision stays, marked withdrawn with the reason.
- Record only non-trivial decisions that are likely to matter outside this file.
  Do not log every small choice you made yourself.
- When presenting open options, mark the optional sections and recommend one.
  Every resolved open option gets a Decisions entry; collapse the discarded option into a "would have" summary.
- Refer to a decision as `{identifier} Decision {number}`, where the identifier is `intent/{number}_{slug}`
  for intents and `{number}_{slug}` (no state) for plans, always without the `.md` suffix,
  e.g. `023.4_init_oob_and_bootstrap Decision 2`.
  Use this format in plans, code, and chat.
- Older files predating this convention are not edited: resolve references to their decisions through the index.

Planner branches: before writing anything for a planner prompt about plan `{input_plan}` (e.g. `005_foo`):
- Stay on the current `plan/*` branch if it is related to the input plan: it is `plan/{input_plan}`,
  its sequence number is a dotted-numbering ancestor of the input plan's (`005.2` on `plan/005_foo`),
  or the input plan's file exists on this branch but not on `main` (it was created here, e.g. by `subdivide`).
- Otherwise commit pending changes on the current branch (stop and ask if it is unclear where they belong),
  then check out `plan/{input_plan}` if it exists, else create it from `main`.
- On `main`, create the branch already for `ask`, so decisions are recorded on it.
- `architecture` and `mvp` use `plan/002_architecture`.
- `todo` merges whatever branch the planner has checked out; never leave unrelated work on it.
