This project is out-of-band: the worktree root is not a git repository. The planner branch rule applies to the
`docs` repo (run git inside `docs/`). The planner never creates or switches branches in code repos.

Every worktree (`canon`, `planner`, `builder`, `reviewer`) has this layout:

```
{{ '%-24s '|format('<worktree>/') }}not a git repo
{{ '%-24s '|format('  AGENTS.md') }}generated; do not edit
{{ '%-24s '|format('  .venv/') }}shared virtual env for all repos
{{ '%-24s '|format('  docs/') }}git repo: plans in docs/plans/, body_of_agents.md, bootstrap_venv.sh
{% for repo in code_repos %}
{{ '%-24s '|format('  ' ~ repo.name ~ '/') }}git repo: code (pre-commit hook {{ 'runs on check/accept' if repo.run_hook else 'does not run' }})
{% endfor %}
```

Every plan file in the `implementing` or `checking` state must carry a `branches` frontmatter block
naming, for every code repo, either the branch/base it uses there or `null` if the plan
doesn't touch that repo:

```yaml
---
branches:
{% for repo in code_repos %}
  {{ repo.name }}: {branch: NAME, base: REF}
{% endfor %}
---
```

Use `null` instead of `{branch: NAME, base: REF}` for any repo the plan does not change.
New intents start with a `branches` block that defaults every code repo to `feat/{intent name}`.
When writing tickets from an intent, verify that the block is consistent with the intent;
carry the block over if it is, else ask the user to confirm.
When subdividing, rename each `feat/{intent name}` branch to the ticket's own name.
